COMMIT 8105b0fee0e8babd2664d6c9f78b19a682d64c48
docs(findings): F-30 settles D-049 (d), and measures how far its pattern reaches

FIX-136 could not start without this: practice contradicting a recorded
decision is never a silent fix. The question the row left open was whether
D-049 (d)'s "never an escaped exception" means every throwable or only
RuntimeException. It means every throwable, D-049 needs no amendment, and the
proof is inside the method rather than in an argument about wording: Delivery
:296 and :312 already catch Throwable, and their comments use D-049 (d)'s own
phrase "never an exception out of apply" and say "an Error too". The same
author, in the same method, applied the broad reading twice and wrote it into
the comments, then wrote the save's catch narrower. A decision scoped to
RuntimeException would not look like that; an oversight looks exactly like it.

F-30 is recorded as corrected rather than as written, because its first draft
was wrong in a way that would have cost a round. It implied the permit leak
follows from Delivery.apply calling mode.deliver with no try or finally. That
is true of apply and false of the modes: sync has its own finally at :177-181
and at-most-once at :219-222, so both return the permit, and only
at-least-once has no finally anywhere on its path. The consequence is therefore
one mode's, and it is the consequence that matters -- one permit leaked per
Error until the flow refuses every message while nothing is in flight, and a
stop() that can never complete because the flight never leaves. A lost reply
costs one message, which the sender retries. A leaked permit costs the flow.
The FIX-136 row's Done-when is rewritten to the per-mode form for the same
reason: that cell is what binds the test-author, so a half-truth there is not a
documentation defect but a wrong instruction.

The addendum answers the generalisation F-30 itself made. "Every one of those
comments is a candidate site" was written from four sites, so all 165 catch
clauses in flottila-*/src/main/java were read. Two new candidates, not a long
tail; nine that cannot be settled by reading, six of those resolvable by one
structural question; about 102 type-specific catches that are not this family
at all. So FIX-136 is not the tip of an iceberg, and nobody needs to sweep for
this again. FIX-142 takes the strongest straggler, Parking.java:86, which is
strongest for where it sits rather than what it catches: an Error from the blob
store escapes park(), and park() is called inside wrap's catch (Exception), so
it skips the park-failure handling entirely -- the DLQ failing to record that
it could not record. FIX-143 takes the nine for triage. FIX-141 takes
Producers.java:49, deliberately out of FIX-136 because it is a shutdown path.

Packets for FIX-136, FIX-137 and FIX-138 ride along. FIX-137's decision is the
architect's and is stated there: keep takeLocked's loop and fix the javadoc,
never narrow it to an if, because a future violation of the invariant then
degrades into extra batches rather than into items queued with no commit in
flight and no window -- a silent stall, the worst failure this class has. Part
of the FIX-137 row is struck rather than deleted: the .reversed() it names is
no longer in the file, so the record keeps what was claimed and what disproved
it. FIX-138's test is green the moment it is written, so its packet requires
the red to come from the only honest place available -- the case must fail when
the throwing cancel() is disabled, or it asserts nothing.

NOTES gains the Recording entries and a new mutant-runs section. Both describe
FIX-139 and FIX-140, which land in the next two commits; they are here because
splitting one file across three commits buys nothing.

